// Configuration for the Verilator SoC harness (sim/verilator_soc.cpp).
//
// Two jobs, both narrow on purpose.
//
// ---- 1. The verdict word ----
//
// sim/tb_sdramboot.v reads its pass/fail word out of block RAM with a
// hierarchical reference (`DUT.RAM.mem[0]`), deliberately: the verdict must
// not live in the memory under test, or a broken SDRAM could read back
// "PASS" by luck. The C++ harness has to reach the same word, and Verilator
// optimises internal signals away unless told not to.
//
// `--public-flat-rd` on the command line would do it for *every* signal in
// the design, which costs simulation speed - the whole point of this build.
// These name the two arrays that are actually touched.
//
// ---- 2. The images ----
//
// The Verilog testbenches preload memory with $readmemh through the same
// kind of hierarchical reference. The harness does it by writing these
// arrays directly before reset is released, which is why the ROM is here
// too even though nothing reads it back. `rd` rather than `rw` because the
// distinction governs VPI access, not the plain C++ member access used
// here; what the directive is needed for is stopping the optimiser from
// dissolving the array.
//
// ---- 3. Where a firmware image stopped ----
//
// A program that produces no console output has either not started or is
// stuck, and those want opposite fixes. The PC and the trap CSRs are what
// separate them, and they cost nothing to expose: an address printed at the
// end of a run goes straight into `addr2line` against the firmware's ELF and
// names the function. This exists because OpenSBI hangs before it can bring
// up a console, so the machine has to say what the firmware cannot.
//
// ---- 4. Waivers ----
//
// None yet. If something rtl/ does legitimately gets refused, waive it here
// with a comment saying why, rather than by weakening the lint on the
// command line where it would apply to code nobody has read.
//
// (Careful: a `//` comment whose first word is the simulator's own name is
// parsed as a pragma, not as prose. That is why none of these lines start
// with it.)
`verilator_config

public_flat_rd -module "wb_ram" -var "mem"
public_flat_rd -module "wb_rom" -var "mem"
// `pc` is the *fetch* PC and is speculative: the BTB predicts, the pipeline
// fetches, and a mispredict squashes it. A probe watching it reports
// addresses the machine never executed - which it duly did, sending one
// debugging round after a branch that was fetched and thrown away. What the
// probes want is the *retired* instruction, so they use id_ex_pc qualified by
// instret_retire instead. `pc` is kept only for the trace's own use.
// Both cores, because both get built: `make verify` runs the in-order one and
// CI matrixes over both, so a .vlt naming only `cpu_core` compiles fine
// locally and fails the wide core's leg of CI with "no member named
// soc_top__DOT__CPU__DOT__instret_retire". It did exactly that.
//
// The C++ needs no #ifdef: Verilator's flattened names come from the
// *instance* path, and both cores are instantiated as `CPU` with a register
// file called `RF`, so the symbols are identical either way. Only the module
// names differ, and those only appear here.
//
// One asymmetry worth knowing when reading a trace from the wide core: it
// dual-issues, and `instret_retire`/`id_ex_pc` describe slot 0 only. Slot 1
// retires through `instret1_retire`, so a branch trace taken from the wide
// core is a sample of control flow rather than all of it.
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "pc"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "id_ex_pc"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "instret_retire"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "pc"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "id_ex_pc"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "instret_retire"
public_flat_rd -module "csr_file" -var "mcause_r"
public_flat_rd -module "csr_file" -var "mepc_r"
public_flat_rd -module "csr_file" -var "mtval_r"
public_flat_rd -module "regfile" -var "regs"
public_flat_rd -module "regfile_wide" -var "regs"
// ---- 5. The shared bus, for checking what memory hands back ----
//
// Every completed transfer on the interconnect's slave side: the address, the
// data, whether it was a write, and which master owns it. The harness has the
// SDRAM's contents, so with these it can check that a read returned the word
// the part actually holds - on every access, for a whole boot.
//
// That is a different question from the ones the probes above answer. They
// say where the machine went; this says whether what it read was real. It
// exists because a Linux boot failed in a way that looked like memory
// returning different data for two identical traversals, and every cheaper
// check - the blob on disk, the image in memory, the page tables - came back
// clean.
public_flat_rd -module "wb_interconnect" -var "s_adr"
public_flat_rd -module "wb_interconnect" -var "s_we"
public_flat_rd -module "wb_interconnect" -var "fin_ack"
public_flat_rd -module "wb_interconnect" -var "fin_dat"
public_flat_rd -module "wb_interconnect" -var "sel_m1"
public_flat_rd -module "wb_interconnect" -var "sel_m2"

// And the instruction the core actually consumes, which the bus check above
// cannot see: a fetch that hits in the I-cache never reaches the bus at all,
// so `fin_dat` says nothing about it. These three are the cache's *output*
// and the address it was asked for, which is the last point before the word
// becomes an instruction.
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_wb" -var "imem_addr"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_wb" -var "imem_rdata"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_wb" -var "ibus_wait"

// ---- 6. Every translation, against a walk of the same tables ----
//
// With the bus and the fetch path both proved to deliver the word memory
// holds, a wrong *address* is what is left. There is nothing in software that
// can check one: the whole point of translation is that the program does not
// see it. The harness can, because it has the page tables - satp names the
// root and the tables are in the SDRAM it is modelling - so it can walk them
// itself and compare.
//
// Both cores instantiate these as IMMU and MMU, so the C++ paths are the same
// either way; only the module name here is shared.
public_flat_rd -module "mmu" -var "va"
public_flat_rd -module "mmu" -var "satp_ppn"
public_flat_rd -module "mmu" -var "resolved"
public_flat_rd -module "mmu" -var "fault"
public_flat_rd -module "mmu" -var "pa"
public_flat_rd -module "mmu" -var "req"
// `va` is *live* - for a data access it is op1+imm, recomputed from forwarding
// every cycle - and mmu.v answers a concluded walk from the `va_r` it latched
// when the walk started, precisely because the live one decays underneath a
// stall. A checker that compares `pa` against a walk of the live `va` is
// therefore wrong on exactly the cycles a walk finishes, and reports hundreds
// of disagreements that are its own. It did. `state` says which of the two
// the hardware used: S_IDLE (0) means a TLB hit answered from `va`, anything
// else means a walk answered from `va_r`. docs/practices.md section 27.
public_flat_rd -module "mmu" -var "va_r"
public_flat_rd -module "mmu" -var "state"

// ---- 7. The pipeline, for a window of cycles ----
//
// +pipetrace prints what retired and what wrote back, cycle by cycle, over a
// range. It exists because the remaining Linux failure is a register write
// that does not arrive between two adjacent instructions, 33 million cycles
// into a boot - too deep for a waveform (an unwindowed VCD of that run once
// filled a 228 GB disk) and too fine for anything the probes above report.
//
// Both cores carry all of these under the same names, so one C++ path serves
// both, as with the probes above.
// The instruction the decoder is looking at, alongside the PC it is
// attributed to. Those two coming apart is exactly the failure that a probe
// on either one alone cannot see.
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "if_id_valid"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "if_id_valid"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "if_id_instr"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "if_id_pc"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "id_ex_reg_we"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "if_id_instr"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "if_id_pc"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "id_ex_reg_we"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "mem_wb_rd"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "mem_wb_wb_data"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "mem_wb_reg_we"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "redirect_valid"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "mispredict"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "id_ex_pred_taken"
public_flat_rd -module "cpu_core" -var "id_ex_pred_target"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "mem_wb_rd"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "mem_wb_wb_data"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "mem_wb_reg_we"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "redirect_valid"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "mispredict"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "id_ex_pred_taken"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "id_ex_pred_target"

// What rate the UART is *actually* running at. A driver that programs
// the ns16550 divisor changes it out from under `+uart_clks`, and a
// mismatch prints garbage - which looks like a firmware fault rather
// than a decoding one.
public_flat_rd -module "uart" -var "divisor_r"

// And what the program handed the transmitter, which the decoded line above
// cannot tell you on its own.
//
// rtl/uart.v holds one byte. Its TX_IDLE arm takes a write only when the
// transmitter is free (`write_thr && !tx_busy`); a write that arrives while a
// character is still being shifted out is discarded, silently, because that is
// what a part with no FIFO can do and there is no status bit for "I threw the
// last one away". From software the result is indistinguishable from a console
// decoding at the wrong rate.
//
// A driver that polls LSR.THRE before each byte never provokes it. One that
// believes it has a sixteen-byte FIFO writes sixteen after a single poll and
// provokes it fifteen times per burst.
//
// `tx_state` rather than the `tx_busy` wire it feeds: a one-line continuous
// assignment is exactly what the optimiser folds away, and the state register
// cannot be.
public_flat_rd -module "uart" -var "write_thr"
public_flat_rd -module "uart" -var "tx_state"
public_flat_rd -module "uart" -var "wdata"

// ---- 8. the wide core's second issue slot ----
//
// `+checkdecode` reads if_id_valid / if_id_pc / if_id_instr, and on a
// dual-issue core those name **slot 0 only**. The note in section 5 above
// records that as a caveat about reading a *trace* from the wide core; it is
// really a hole in the strongest check this harness has, and it took a defect
// that only the wide core exhibits to make the difference matter - a Linux
// boot that runs the whole kernel correctly and then fails `execve` with
// -EFAULT, with 52.8 million decoded instructions all matching their PC.
//
// "Half the machine" is how this was described before it was measured, and
// that was an assumption. Adding slot 1 takes a wide-core Linux boot from
// 52,823,404 checked instructions to 56,629,310 - about 7% more, because the
// second slot only ever issues one class of single-cycle ALU op and most
// cycles have nothing for it. Still a hole, and still exactly the part that
// exists only on the core that fails; just a seventh of the machine rather
// than a half.
//
// Slot 1 is formed at issue rather than at IF/ID, so its checkpoint is the
// ID/EX register instead. `id_ex1_instr` is carried for observability only -
// nothing in the datapath reads it - which is exactly what makes it the right
// thing to compare against memory.
//
// core_ooo only. sim/verilator_soc.cpp guards the check with CORE_OOO, and
// the Makefile now passes that to the C++ compiler as well as to Verilog.
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "id_ex1_valid"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "id_ex1_pc"
public_flat_rd -module "core_ooo" -var "id_ex1_instr"
